# Agent Clients

An Agent Client connects Pontia to a coding agent's native session, control channel, and conversation history. The agent still runs locally with its own credentials, configuration, tools, and filesystem permissions.

Pontia includes these Agent Clients:

| Client | Local requirement | Local integration |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Pi](/docs/agent-clients/pi) | `pi` and `tmux` | Pontia's Pi plugin |
| [Codex](/docs/codex) | A Codex CLI with app-server daemon support | Per-user Codex daemon startup, launcher, and shell PATH |
| [Claude Code](/docs/agent-clients/claude) | Node.js LTS, npm, and Claude Code authentication | The `@pontia/claude-client` package from npm |
| Google Antigravity | `agy` | Enable in Agent Clients (ACP) |
| Grok Build | `grok` | Enable in Agent Clients (ACP) |
| Kiro | `kiro-cli` | Sign in with `kiro-cli login` |

Choose Antigravity or Kiro for a Bot, memory retrieval, or dreaming to use its restricted agent with file reading, terminal commands, and web search. Helpers working in a Workspace use the selected client’s usual tools and permission prompts.

## Choose Clients during initialization

Run:

```sh
pontia init
```

The initializer checks your selected Clients before applying the configuration. Pi is selected by default; Codex and `claude-agent` are optional. Selected Clients are enabled for new Sessions.

## Use Kiro in a Session

Install `kiro-cli` on the PATH of the user running Pontia and run `kiro-cli login` as that user. Enable Kiro in **Settings → Agent Clients** or run `pontia client enable kiro`.

Select Kiro and a model when creating a Session. You can approve a tool request for one use, steer a running turn when Kiro offers steering, queue a follow-up for the next turn, and resume a conversation with its timeline.

You can view estimated context usage, compact an idle Session, and see automatic and manual compaction checkpoints in the timeline.

Kiro follows Session instructions for sharing files as attachments. You can assign a Kiro Agent Profile with a system prompt to a Workflow node.

<h2 id="enable-an-acp-agent">Enable Grok Build</h2>

Install Grok Build so that `grok` is on the Pontia service user’s PATH. Pontia launches it with `grok agent stdio`. Enable Grok Build under **Settings → Agent Clients** in the Dashboard, or run:

```sh
pontia client enable grok
```

In an idle or interrupted Grok session, use `/compact` in the Dashboard to compress the conversation context. You can continue in the same session and see compaction checkpoints in its timeline.

Choose Grok Build for a Bot, a helper, or dreaming. In these Sessions, Grok keeps its terminal and read tools. Its dedicated tools for file edits, media output, worktrees, subagents, workflows, scheduling, background tasks, monitoring, and MCP tool calls are disabled. Bots, retrieval helpers, and dreaming also turn off automatic skill discovery.

Send a message to a busy Grok Session, Bot, or helper to add instructions at the next tool or model transition. Grok finishes its current work. If the turn has passed its last transition, Grok runs the message as a follow-up turn.

Run `pontia setup pi`, `pontia setup codex`, or `pontia setup claude` to install or repair a local integration. Omit the Client to select interactively. Setup also enables the Client.

In the Dashboard, open **Settings → Agent Clients**, select Pi, Codex, or Claude Code, and click **Initialize** to perform the same setup on that device. The page shows the latest setup output and result, including after reconnecting.

## Client capabilities differ

The Dashboard presents the controls supported by the selected Client, such as interruption, history branching, and queued delivery.

A Pontia Session is durably associated with one client-native conversation identity after binding. Reopening the same native conversation continues the existing Session; a new native conversation belongs to a new Session.

## Client credentials and permissions

Each Client uses its own credentials, sandbox, and local permissions. Sign in to each Client and verify that it can perform the intended work before starting the integration.

When creating a Session through `POST /api/v1/sessions`, set `execution_policy` to select tools, exclude tools, disable MCP, or choose its permission mode:

```json
{
  "client_type": "pi",
  "workspace": "/path/to/registered/workspace",
  "execution_policy": {
    "disabled_tools": ["edit", "write"],
    "disable_mcp": true,
    "yolo": true
  }
}
```

Pi and Claude Code accept `tools` and `disabled_tools` using their native, case-sensitive tool names. For example, use `tools: ["read", "bash", "grep"]` for Pi or `tools: ["Read", "Bash", "Glob"]` for Claude Code. Omitting `tools` keeps the Client's normal selection. `disabled_tools` excludes tools even when they appear in `tools`; for Claude Code, an example is `["Edit", "Write", "NotebookEdit", "Agent"]`. Pi accepts native wildcard patterns, and Claude Code accepts native deny rules such as `Bash(git push *)`.

Tool selection follows the Client's native behavior: an empty `tools` list disables Pi tools or Claude Code built-in tools. MCP is controlled separately. Set `disable_mcp: true` to prevent MCP connections and tools in Pi or Claude Code; omitting it leaves the Client's MCP configuration in effect. Disabling an editing tool does not prevent file changes through Bash.

Pi, Claude Code, and Codex accept `yolo: true` to run without Client approval prompts or Client sandboxing. Pi already runs tools this way. Omitting `yolo` keeps the Client's normal permission behavior. These settings apply to the Session and persist across restart, resume, and history forks. Requests for a policy the selected Client cannot enforce are rejected before Session creation.

See [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting) when a Client is missing, cannot start, or stops reporting output.
